<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bertolt Brecht</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1898">1898</a>–<a href="/years/1956">1956</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Bert Brecht, Eugen Berthold Friedrich Brecht</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bertolt Brecht was a German <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> practitioner, playwright and poet. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>, where he moved in 1924, he wrote The Threepenny Opera with Elisabeth Hauptmann and Kurt Weill, began a lifelong collaboration with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanns-eisler">Hanns Eisler</a> and became a leading theorist of epic theatre, which sought the audience's critical reflection rather than its emotional identification. He fled Nazi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> in 1933, first to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scandinavia">Scandinavia</a> and later to Southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/california">California</a>, and in exile wrote Life of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galileo-galilei">Galileo</a> and Mother Courage and Her Children. Questioned by the House Un-American Activities Committee in 1947, he returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and co-founded the Berliner Ensemble in East Berlin with Helene Weigel.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bertolt-brecht.jpg" alt="Bertolt Brecht" width="330" height="477" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kolbe, Jörg · CC BY-SA 3.0 de · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bertolt-Brecht.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Bertolt Brecht</b>, Eugene O’Neill and later Samuel Beckett shaped the theatre, and Frank Lloyd Wright, Le Corbusier and Ludwig Mies van der Rohe its architecture.</blockquote><a href="/movement/modernism">Modernism · People</a></li><li><blockquote>The &quot;Einheitsfrontlied&quot; (United Front Song) is one of the best-known songs of the German labour movement, written by <b>Bertolt Brecht</b> and set to music by Hanns Eisler.</blockquote><a href="/songs/einheitsfrontlied">Einheitsfrontlied</a></li><li><blockquote>The &quot;Solidaritätslied&quot; (Solidarity Song) is a revolutionary workers' song written between 1929 and 1931 by <b>Bertolt Brecht</b> and set to music by Hanns Eisler.</blockquote><a href="/songs/solidaritatslied">Solidaritätslied</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augsburg">Augsburg</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in East Berlin, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.37,10.9&amp;year=1935" aria-label="Augsburg on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4803 472 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Bertolt Brecht"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5253 742h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5253 742h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.37,10.9&amp;year=1935">What was here in 1935? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
